[[Image:Jupiter and its shrunken Great Red Spot.jpg|thumb|The planet Jupiter]]
[[File:Jupiter symbol (outline).svg|thumb|Jupiter's planetary symbol {{angbr|[[♃]]}}]]

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dyew-||*peh₂-}}
From {{der|en|la|Iuppiter|Iūpiter|t=father Jove}}, from {{der|en|itc-pro|*djous patēr|lit=sky father}} (cognate with {{cog|grc|Ζεύς πάτερ|Ζεῦ πάτερ|t=father Zeus}}), from {{m|itc-pro|*djous|t=day, sky}} + {{m|itc-pro|*patēr|t=father}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dyḗws|lit=the bright one}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*dyew-|t=to be bright, day sky}}, and {{m|ine-pro|*ph₂tḗr|t=father}}. {{doublet|en|Dyaus Pita}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈd͡ʒupɪtɚ/|[ˈd͡ʒupɪɾɚ]|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-Jupiter.ogg|a=US}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q319}} {{lb|en|astronomy}} The fifth and by far the largest [[planet]] in the [[Solar System]], a [[gas giant]], represented by the symbol [[♃]] in [[astronomy]]. Jupiter is known for its [[Great Red Spot]] and many [[moons]] including the [[Galilean moons]].
# {{senseid|en|Q4649}} {{lb|en|Roman god}} The King of the Gods, also called [[Jove]]. Equivalent to the Greek [[Zeus]], Jupiter was one of the children of [[Saturn]]. As supreme god of the Roman pantheon, Jupiter was the god of thunder, lightning, and storms, and appropriately called the god of light and sky.
# {{lb|en|informal}} The largest of a group of things or a region.
#: {{ux|en|Brazil could be considered the '''Jupiter''' of South America.}}
# {{place|en|number of places|c/USA}}:
## {{place|en|unincorporated community|co/Tuolumne County|s/California}}, named after a mine.
## {{place|en|town|co/Palm Beach County|s/Florida}}, named in error after the Roman god.
## {{place|en|twp|co/Kittson County|s/Minnesota}}, named after the planet.
## {{place|en|unincorporated community|co/Buncombe County|s/North Carolina}}.
# {{place|en|summer resort|on the|sea/Black Sea|in|c/Romania}}.

====Usage notes====
* The planet Jupiter usually takes the adjectival form {{m|en|Jovian}}, and rarely {{m|en|Jupiterian}}. The [[demonym]] of the [[planet]] is also {{m|en|Jovian}}.

